  "fault_path": {
    "id": "owned-vfilememchunk-map-unmap-round-trip",
    "distinct_from_completed_witnesses": [
      "owned VFileMemChunk growth and shrink/regrow",
      "borrowed-memory seek and short-write boundaries",
      "anonymous backing allocation failure",
      "VFileMemChunk wrapper allocation failure"
    ],
    "source_contract": "The public VFile has void map/unmap callbacks. Pinned _vfmMap returns the owned backing pointer when requested size is within the logical size and NULL otherwise. _vfmUnmap ignores all arguments and does not release or mutate the backing memory; _vfmCloseFree releases the owned backing on close.",
    "source_lines": [
      "include/mgba-util/vfs.h:40-50",
      "include/mgba-util/vfs.h:83-85",
      "src/util/vfs/vfs-mem.c:90-118",
      "src/util/vfs/vfs-mem.c:146-152",
      "src/util/vfs/vfs-mem.c:273-288"
    ],
    "production_caller": {
      "source": "src/core/rewind.c:30-32,94-120",
      "contract": "mCore rewind owns two VFileMemChunk instances, resizes logical extents as needed, maps both states for diffing, and calls unmap on each mapped region.",
      "compiled_or_executed": false
    },
    "finding": "On the tested Linux host with unchanged pinned memory.c and vfs-mem.c, a valid mapping can be unmapped and the VFile remains readable and writable; a fresh map observes the write, and an over-extent map returns NULL. This characterizes the memory backend callback sequence only, not mCore rewind execution or a saved game."
  },
